  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on small probability outcomes (less than 20%) and adjusts their weight upward by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the 0 Euro outcome has a 10% probability, which is below 20%, so it would receive a weighted probability of 20% (10% + 10%). The -3000 Euro outcome has a 90% probability, which is not adjusted. In Lottery B, the 0 Euro outcome has a 55% probability (not adjusted), and the -6000 Euro outcome has a 45% probability (also not adjusted, since it's above 20%). Only the 0 Euro outcome in Lottery A qualifies for the probability weighting. The reason evaluates each lottery based on the sum of outcomes weighted by their (adjusted) probabilities and prefers the one with the more favorable (i.e., less negative or higher) expected value under this transformation. Thus, the relevant information is the identification of outcomes with probabilities <20%, their upward reweighting, and comparison of the resulting weighted outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports considering the probabilities and outcomes of both lotteries. They state that they preferred Lottery B because they perceived -6000 Euros with 45% as more favorable than -3000 Euros with 90%. They also note that in the best case, both lotteries yield 0 Euros, but Lottery B has a 55% chance of this outcome versus only 10% for Lottery A, so they chose B. The individual explicitly compares the chances of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros), emphasizing the higher probability of the zero outcome in B (55%) versus A (10%). Their justification centers on the higher chance of the non-loss outcome in B, even though both can result in zero. They do not mention any transformation of probabilities, especially not an increase in the weight of low-probability events, nor do they indicate special attention to the 10% outcome in A. Instead, they focus on the relative size of the losses and the higher probability of avoiding loss in B.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires focusing on outcomes with less than 20% probability and increasing their weight. Only the 0 Euro outcome in Lottery A (10%) qualifies. This would lead to giving more weight to the 0 Euro outcome in A than in B. However, the individual does not show any indication of focusing on low-probability events or adjusting their weights. Instead, they emphasize the higher probability of the zero outcome in B (55%) and contrast it with the low chance in A (10%), suggesting they treated probabilities at face value or even discounted low-probability gains. The individual’s reasoning moves in the opposite direction: they favor B because its better outcome (0 Euros) is more likely, not because they overweighted the small chance of 0 in A. There is no alignment with the reason’s core mechanism—probability transformation for rare events. The information considered (probabilities of zero outcomes, magnitude of losses) partially overlaps, but the processing rule (upweighting small probabilities) is absent in the report. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
